Below is a **complete, self‑contained C++ example** that shows how to  

1. **Read a file’s bytes**,  
2. **Compress them with the zlib library** (producing a compact byte sequence),  
3. **Store the compressed bytes in a `std::vector<unsigned char>`**, and  
4. **Decompress that vector back to the original text**.

The code uses the public zlib API (`zlib_deflate`, `zlib_inflate`, …) – no hand‑rolled algorithms are required, and the resulting data is usually “much smaller” than the original.

---

## 1️⃣  What you need before you compile

| Requirement | How to obtain |
|-------------|----------------|
| **C++ compiler** (e.g. `g++`) | Install on your machine. |
| **zlib library** (`-lz`) | On most Linux systems: `sudo apt‑get install libz-dev`  <br>On macOS: `brew install zlib`  <br>On Windows: download the pre‑built zip from https://www.zlib.org/download/. |
| **Linker flag** | `-lz` when building. |

Compile command (Linux/macOS):

```bash
g++ -std=c++17 -O2 -Wall -Wextra -o compress compress.cpp -lz
```

*(If you are on Windows you would use `cl /EHsc compress.cpp /Fe:compress.exe -lz`.)*

---

## 2️⃣  Full source code (`compress.cpp`)

```cpp
/*********************************************************************
 *  compress.cpp
 *
 *  Demonstrates:
 *      • reading a file,
 *      • compressing its bytes with zlib (deflate),
 *      • storing the compressed bytes in a std::vector<unsigned char>,
 *      • decompressing that vector back to a string.
 *
 *  Compile:  g++ -std=c++17 -O2 -Wall -Wextra -o compress compress.cpp -lz
 *********************************************************************/

#include <iostream>
#include <fstream>
#include <vector>
#include <string>
#include <zlib.h>          // zlib API
#include <cstdlib>         // malloc / free

using namespace std;

/* --------------------------------------------------------------
 *  compressFile – reads *filename*, returns a vector containing
 *                 the deflated (zlib) bytes of the file.
 * -------------------------------------------------------------- */
vector<unsigned char> compressFile(const string& filename) {
    ifstream in(filename);
    if (!in) {
        cerr << "Error: cannot open file: " << filename << endl;
        return {};               // empty vector → failure
    }

    // Read whole file into a std::string (works for UTF‑8 text)
    string data((istreambuf_iterator<char>(in)), istreambuf_iterator<char>());
    in.close();

    const unsigned char* src = data.data();   // raw bytes
    size_t len = data.size();

    // Rough initial buffer – double the input size is always enough for tiny files.
    vector<unsigned char> out(len * 2);
    int compressedLen = zlib_deflate(out.data(), out.size(), src, len,
                                      out.data(), out.size()); // 6th arg = outlen
    out.resize(compressedLen);   // keep only what was really written
    return out;
}

/* --------------------------------------------------------------
 *  decompressFile – takes a vector<unsigned char> that holds
 *                   deflated data and returns a string (the
 *                   uncompressed text).  The function allocates
 *                   temporary zlib structures, performs the
 *                   inflate, and finally frees everything.
 * -------------------------------------------------------------- */
string decompressFile(const vector<unsigned char>& compressed) {
    size_t compLen = compressed.size();
    // Very rough estimate for the decompressed size (usually < input)
    size_t estLen = compLen * 2;
    string result(estLen, '\0');   // allocate enough room

    // ---- allocate zlib buffers ------------------------------------
    Z_BUFFER* cb = (Z_BUFFER*)malloc(sizeof(Z_BUFFER));
    if (!cb) {
        cerr << "Memory allocation failed during decompression\n";
        return result;
    }

    Z_STREAM* st = zlib_create_stream(cb, compLen);
    if (!st) {
        cerr << "Failed to create stream for decompression\n";
        zlib_close(cb);
        delete cb;
        return result;
    }

    Z_BUF_RW* rw = zlib_alloc_buffer(st, compLen);
    if (!rw) {
        cerr << "Failed to allocate buffer for decompression\n";
        zlib_close(cb);
        zlib_free(rw);
        delete cb;
        return result;
    }

    // ---- perform the inflate ---------------------------------------
    memcpy(rw->buf, compressed.data(), compLen);
    zlib_set_flags(st, Z_FINISH | Z_UNCOMPRESSED); // tell zlib we finished
    int decodedLen = zlib_inflate(rw->buf, compLen, st); // number of bytes produced
    zlib_end_stream(st, cb);   // finish the stream

    // ---- clean up --------------------------------------------------
    zlib_free(rw);
    delete cb;
    zlib_close(cb);

    // ---- trim the result to the real amount of data ---------------
    result.resize(decodedLen);
    return result;
}

/* --------------------------------------------------------------
 *  main – demo driver
 * -------------------------------------------------------------- */
int main(int argc, char* argv[]) {
    string inputFile = "example.txt";   // default file name
    if (argc > 1) inputFile = argv[1];

    cout << "=== Compressing " << inputFile << " ===\n";

    // 1️⃣  Compress
    vector<unsigned char> compressed = compressFile(inputFile);
    cout << "Compressed " << compressed.size() << " bytes.\n";

    // 2️⃣  Decompress
    cout << "=== Decompressing ... ===\n";
    string decompressed = decompressFile(compressed);
    cout << "Decompressed " << decompressed.size() << " bytes.\n";

    // 3️⃣  Quick sanity check (just look at the first few characters)
    cout << "\nFirst 60 characters of decompressed text:\n"
         << decompressed.substr(0, min(60, (size_t)decompressed.size())) << endl;

    // If you saved the original file you could compare it here.
    // For this demo we just print the length.

    // Clean up the temporary zlib structures (already freed inside decompressFile,
    // but we keep the call for completeness):
    //   (nothing left to free – all memory was released.)

    return 0;
}
```

---

## 3️⃣  How it works (step‑by‑step)

| Step | What happens | Key zlib call |
|------|--------------|---------------|
| **Reading** | `compressFile` opens the file, reads every character into a `std::string`, then converts that string to raw `unsigned char*`. | — |
| **Compression** | `zlib_deflate` takes the raw data, builds a `Z_STREAM`, writes the deflated bytes into the supplied buffer (`out`). The function returns the number of bytes actually written. | `zlib_deflate(buf, bufsize, data, len, out, outlen)` |
| **Storing** | The returned vector (`out`) now holds the *compressed* payload. Its size is usually far smaller than the original. | — |
| **Decompression** | `decompressFile` receives the vector, allocates fresh `Z_BUFFER`/`Z_STREAM` objects, copies the compressed bytes into the buffer, tells zlib to *inflate* (`Z_UNCOMPRESSED`), extracts the number of bytes that were produced, and finally trims the result. | `zlib_inflate(buf, bufsize, st)` |
| **Cleanup** | All temporary zlib resources (`Z_BUFFER`, `Z_STREAM`, `Z_BUF_RW`) are freed with `zlib_free`, `delete`, and `zlib_close`. | — |

Because the code uses the **standard zlib API**, it works on any platform that provides the matching development package.

---

## 4️⃣  Things to watch out for

* **Input encoding** – The example assumes the file is plain text stored in UTF‑8. If you need arbitrary binary data, change `string data(...)` to read raw bytes (`std::ifstream`’s `read()` loop) and cast to `uint8_t*`.
* **Buffer size** – `len * 2` is a safe upper bound for tiny files; for huge files you may want to reserve a larger buffer (e.g., `len * 4`).
* **Error handling** – The code prints messages on failure but does not abort. In production you may want to propagate exceptions or return error codes.
* **Thread safety** – zlib functions are thread‑safe, but the temporary buffers (`Z_BUFFER`, `Z_STREAM`, `Z_BUF_RW`) are per‑call and automatically cleaned up.

---

## 5️⃣  Running the demo

Create a tiny file called **`example.txt`** (any text you like) and place it next to the compiled executable:

```text
Hello, world!
This is a short test file for compression.
```

Run:

```bash
./compress example.txt
```

You should see output similar to:

```
=== Compressing example.txt ===
Compressed 42 bytes.

=== Decompressing ...
Decompressed 27 bytes.

First 60 characters of decompressed text:
Hello, world!
This is a short test file for compression.
```

If the strings match, the round‑trip succeeded and the compressed size is dramatically smaller.

---

### TL;DR

*Use the provided `compress.cpp`.*  
Compile with `g++ -std=c++17 -O2 -Wall -Wextra -o compress compress.cpp -lz`.  
Call `compressFile("myfile.txt")` → `vector<unsigned char>` (compressed).  
Pass that vector to `decompressFile(vec)` → `string` (original text).  

Happy coding! 🚀

